WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.730 --> 00:00:01.720
- [Narrator] Named for its method

00:00:01.720 --> 00:00:04.600
of burying rotting
carcasses to feed its young,

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:07.600
this is the grave digger
of the animal kingdom,

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:09.527
the American burying beetle.

00:00:09.527 --> 00:00:12.444
(intriguing music)

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:17.340
These beetles feed on
small decomposing animals

00:00:17.340 --> 00:00:18.900
like birds and mice.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.800
Inside their antennae are
strong chemical receptors

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.790
that detect rotting meat.

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:26.540
They have powerful wings and
will travel long distances

00:00:26.540 --> 00:00:27.603
to reach their meal.

00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.210
Males and females work
together to bury the corpse,

00:00:31.210 --> 00:00:34.490
and females will then lay her eggs nearby.

00:00:34.490 --> 00:00:37.890
After the larvae hatch, both
parents tend to their young,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:40.313
a behavior rarely found in insects.

00:00:41.300 --> 00:00:44.200
Once a prolific beetle
found in over 35 states

00:00:44.200 --> 00:00:47.750
across the U.S., today it
can be found in just six.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:50.510
Listed as endangered back in 1989,

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:53.710
the American burying
beetle's future is bleak.

00:00:53.710 --> 00:00:55.610
While conservation efforts are underway,

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:57.483
there is still a long way to go.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:00.353
This is the American burying beetle.

